from quick_lib import *

build("javascript", "JavaScript — Quick Revision", "The questions that come up in almost every JavaScript interview.", [
("must","easy","What is the difference between var, let and const?",
 L("**var** is function-scoped, hoisted and initialised as `undefined`; it can be redeclared.",
   "**let** is block-scoped, hoisted but unusable until its declaration (temporal dead zone); it can be reassigned.",
   "**const** is block-scoped like let, but the binding cannot be reassigned. Objects it points to can still be mutated."),
 C("js","console.log(a); // undefined\nvar a = 1;\nconsole.log(b); // ReferenceError (TDZ)\nlet b = 2;")),
("must","medium","What is a closure? Give a practical use.",
 "A closure is a function that remembers the variables of the scope where it was created, even after that scope has finished executing.",
 C("js","function counter() {\n  let n = 0;\n  return () => ++n;\n}\nconst next = counter();\nnext(); // 1\nnext(); // 2"),
 "Common uses: data privacy, function factories, memoisation, debounce/throttle and event handlers that need remembered state."),
("must","medium","How does `this` work in JavaScript?",
 "`this` is decided by **how a function is called**, not where it is written:",
 L("Method call `obj.fn()` → `this` is `obj`.","Plain call `fn()` → `undefined` in strict mode (global object otherwise).","`call`, `apply`, `bind` set `this` explicitly.","`new Fn()` → the newly created object.","Arrow functions have no own `this`; they use the one from the enclosing scope.")),
("must","medium","Explain the event loop, microtasks and macrotasks.",
 "JavaScript runs on one thread. The call stack executes synchronous code; async work is handed to the browser/Node and its callback is queued. When the stack is empty the event loop picks the next task.",
 L("**Microtasks** (Promise `.then`, `queueMicrotask`) run to completion after the current task, before anything else.","**Macrotasks** (`setTimeout`, `setInterval`, I/O, UI events) run one per loop turn."),
 C("js","console.log(1);\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(2), 0);\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3));\nconsole.log(4);\n// 1 4 3 2")),
("must","easy","What is hoisting?",
 "Declarations are registered during the creation phase of an execution context, so they can be referenced before the line that declares them. Function declarations are fully hoisted; `var` is hoisted as `undefined`; `let`/`const`/`class` are hoisted but stay in the temporal dead zone until declared.",
 C("js","hello(); // works\nfunction hello() { console.log('hi'); }\n\nbye(); // TypeError: bye is not a function\nvar bye = function () {};")),
("must","medium","How do Promises work and what is async/await?",
 "A Promise represents a value that will exist later. It is **pending**, then settles as **fulfilled** or **rejected**. `then/catch/finally` chain follow-up work. `async/await` is syntax on top of promises that makes asynchronous code read top to bottom.",
 C("js","async function load() {\n  try {\n    const res = await fetch('/api/user');\n    return await res.json();\n  } catch (e) {\n    console.error(e);\n  }\n}"),
 N("`await` pauses only the async function, not the whole thread. Independent calls should run together with `Promise.all`.")),
("must","medium","Difference between `==` and `===`?",
 "`===` compares value and type with no conversion. `==` converts operands first (type coercion), which gives surprising results such as `0 == ''` and `null == undefined`. Prefer `===`; use `Object.is` when you need to tell `NaN` and `-0` apart.",
 C("js","'5' == 5;   // true\n'5' === 5;  // false\nnull == undefined;  // true\nnull === undefined; // false\nNaN === NaN; // false")),
("must","medium","Explain prototypal inheritance.",
 "Every object has an internal link ([[Prototype]]) to another object. When a property isn't found on the object, the engine walks up this chain until it finds it or reaches `null`. `class` syntax is sugar over this mechanism.",
 C("js","const animal = { speak() { return 'sound'; } };\nconst dog = Object.create(animal);\ndog.speak(); // found on the prototype\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {}\nObject.getPrototypeOf(Dog.prototype) === Animal.prototype; // true")),
("must","easy","map vs forEach vs filter vs reduce?",
 L("`forEach` runs a function for each item and returns `undefined`.","`map` returns a new array of transformed items.","`filter` returns a new array of items that pass a test.","`reduce` folds the array into a single value (number, object, array…)."),
 C("js","[1,2,3].map(n => n * 2);        // [2,4,6]\n[1,2,3].filter(n => n > 1);     // [2,3]\n[1,2,3].reduce((s, n) => s + n, 0); // 6")),
("must","medium","call, apply and bind — what is the difference?",
 L("`fn.call(ctx, a, b)` invokes immediately with arguments listed.","`fn.apply(ctx, [a, b])` invokes immediately with an array of arguments.","`fn.bind(ctx, a)` returns a **new function** with `this` (and optional leading args) fixed.")),
("good","medium","Shallow copy vs deep copy — how do you clone an object?",
 "A shallow copy duplicates only the top level; nested objects stay shared. Spread (`{...o}`) and `Object.assign` are shallow. For a deep copy use `structuredClone(o)` (handles dates, maps, sets, cycles). `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(o))` works only for plain JSON data and drops functions, `undefined` and dates.",
 C("js","const a = { n: { x: 1 } };\nconst s = { ...a };\ns.n.x = 2;\na.n.x; // 2 — shared!\nconst d = structuredClone(a);")),
("good","medium","What are debounce and throttle?",
 L("**Debounce** waits until calls stop for N ms, then runs once (search-as-you-type).","**Throttle** runs at most once every N ms (scroll, resize)."),
 C("js","function debounce(fn, ms) {\n  let t;\n  return (...args) => {\n    clearTimeout(t);\n    t = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms);\n  };\n}")),
("good","easy","What are arrow functions and how do they differ from normal functions?",
 L("Shorter syntax; implicit return for expressions.","No own `this`, `arguments` or `super` — they use the enclosing ones.","Cannot be used with `new` and have no `prototype`.")),
("good","medium","What is event delegation and bubbling?",
 "Events travel from the target up through its ancestors (**bubbling**). With delegation you attach one listener to a parent and inspect `event.target`, instead of attaching one per child — fewer listeners and it works for elements added later.",
 C("js","list.addEventListener('click', (e) => {\n  const li = e.target.closest('li');\n  if (li) select(li.dataset.id);\n});")),
("good","easy","What are the primitive types in JavaScript?",
 "`string`, `number`, `bigint`, `boolean`, `undefined`, `null` and `symbol`. Everything else is an object. Primitives are immutable and compared by value; objects are compared by reference. Note `typeof null === 'object'` is a long-standing quirk."),
("good","medium","What is destructuring, spread and rest?",
 C("js","const { name, age = 18, ...others } = user;   // object destructuring + rest\nconst [first, , third] = list;             // array destructuring\nconst merged = { ...a, ...b };             // spread\nfunction sum(...nums) {}                   // rest parameters")),
("good","hard","What are generators and iterators?",
 "An iterator is any object with a `next()` method returning `{ value, done }`. A generator function (`function*`) produces an iterator whose body can pause at `yield` and resume later — useful for lazy sequences and custom iteration.",
 C("js","function* ids() {\n  let i = 1;\n  while (true) yield i++;\n}\nconst g = ids();\ng.next().value; // 1\ng.next().value; // 2")),
("good","medium","What is currying and memoisation?",
 L("**Currying** turns `f(a, b)` into `f(a)(b)`, enabling partial application.","**Memoisation** caches results by arguments so repeated calls are instant."),
 C("js","const memo = (fn) => {\n  const cache = new Map();\n  return (n) => cache.has(n) ? cache.get(n) : (cache.set(n, fn(n)), cache.get(n));\n};")),
])

build("react", "React — Quick Revision", "Hooks, rendering and performance — the essentials interviewers ask.", [
("must","easy","What is the Virtual DOM and how does reconciliation work?",
 "The Virtual DOM is a lightweight JavaScript description of the UI. On every update React builds a new tree, **diffs** it against the previous one and applies only the minimal real DOM changes. It assumes different element types produce different trees and uses `key` to match list items.",
 ),
("must","easy","State vs props?",
 L("**Props** are inputs passed from parent to child; read-only inside the child.","**State** is data owned by the component that changes over time and triggers a re-render when updated."),
 N("Data flows down (props), events flow up (callbacks).")),
("must","medium","How does useState work? Why is the update asynchronous?",
 "`useState` returns the current value and a setter. Calling the setter schedules a re-render; React batches updates, so the variable in the current render does not change immediately. When the next value depends on the previous one, use the functional form.",
 C("jsx","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\nsetCount(count + 1);\nsetCount(count + 1);       // still +1 total\nsetCount(c => c + 1);\nsetCount(c => c + 1);      // +2 total")),
("must","medium","Explain useEffect and its dependency array.",
 "`useEffect` runs side effects after render. The dependency array controls when:",
 L("No array → after every render.","`[]` → once after mount.","`[a, b]` → when `a` or `b` change."),
 "Return a cleanup function to remove subscriptions, timers or listeners — it runs before the next effect and on unmount.",
 C("jsx","useEffect(() => {\n  const id = setInterval(tick, 1000);\n  return () => clearInterval(id);\n}, []);")),
("must","easy","Why do lists need a `key` and what makes a good key?",
 "Keys let React match items between renders so it can reorder, add and remove them without recreating everything. Use a stable unique id from your data. Avoid array indexes when the list can be reordered or filtered — it causes wrong state and wasted renders."),
("must","medium","Controlled vs uncontrolled components?",
 L("**Controlled**: form value lives in React state (`value` + `onChange`). Easy validation and conditional logic.","**Uncontrolled**: the DOM keeps the value; you read it with a `ref` when needed. Less code for simple forms.")),
("must","medium","What causes a re-render, and how do you avoid unnecessary ones?",
 "A component re-renders when its state changes, its parent re-renders, or a context it consumes changes. To limit work:",
 L("`React.memo` for pure child components.","`useCallback` / `useMemo` to keep function and object props stable.","Move state down, closer to where it is used.","Split large contexts; virtualise long lists.")),
("must","medium","useMemo vs useCallback vs React.memo?",
 L("`useMemo(fn, deps)` caches a computed **value**.","`useCallback(fn, deps)` caches a **function** reference.","`React.memo(Component)` skips re-rendering when props are shallow-equal."),
 N("They are optimisations, not guarantees. Measure first; memoising everything adds cost.","warning")),
("must","medium","What is useRef used for?",
 "A ref holds a mutable value that **persists across renders without causing one**. Typical uses: accessing DOM nodes (focus, scroll, measure), storing timer ids, and keeping the previous value of something.",
 C("jsx","const inputRef = useRef(null);\n<input ref={inputRef} />\ninputRef.current.focus();")),
("must","medium","What are the Rules of Hooks?",
 L("Call hooks only at the top level — never inside loops, conditions or nested functions.","Call hooks only from React function components or custom hooks."),
 "React relies on the call order of hooks being identical every render to match each hook with its stored state."),
("good","medium","What is Context API and when should you not use it?",
 "Context passes data through the tree without prop drilling (theme, auth user, locale). Every consumer re-renders when the value changes, so it suits slow-changing data. For frequently-changing, complex state prefer a state library or split contexts."),
("good","medium","What is a custom hook?",
 "A function whose name starts with `use` that calls other hooks to share stateful logic between components (not state itself — each caller gets its own).",
 C("jsx","function useLocalStorage(key, initial) {\n  const [v, setV] = useState(() => JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(key)) ?? initial);\n  useEffect(() => localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(v)), [key, v]);\n  return [v, setV];\n}")),
("good","medium","useReducer vs useState?",
 "`useReducer` centralises state transitions in a pure `(state, action) => newState` function. It fits state with several related fields or complex update rules, and makes updates easy to test and log."),
("good","hard","What is lifting state up and prop drilling?",
 "When two siblings need the same data, move it to their closest common parent and pass it down. Passing props through many layers that don't use them is **prop drilling**; solve it with composition (`children`), context, or a store."),
("good","medium","How do you handle code splitting in React?",
 "Load code on demand with `React.lazy` and `Suspense`, typically per route.",
 C("jsx","const Settings = lazy(() => import('./Settings'));\n<Suspense fallback={<Spinner />}>\n  <Settings />\n</Suspense>")),
("good","medium","What are error boundaries?",
 "Class components implementing `getDerivedStateFromError` / `componentDidCatch` that catch errors thrown while rendering their children and show a fallback UI. They do not catch errors in event handlers or async code."),
("good","hard","What does React 18 add — concurrent rendering and automatic batching?",
 L("**Automatic batching**: state updates in timeouts, promises and native events are batched too.","**Transitions** (`startTransition`) mark updates as non-urgent so typing stays responsive.","`useDeferredValue`, Suspense improvements and `createRoot`.")),
("good","medium","Class lifecycle methods vs hooks?",
 L("`componentDidMount` → `useEffect(fn, [])`","`componentDidUpdate` → `useEffect(fn, [deps])`","`componentWillUnmount` → the cleanup returned from `useEffect`")),
])
